@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Components
@using SpaceSails.Core

@* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   #243 · ONE CRITERION, DRAWN ONCE, WHEREVER A GATE IS BEING READ.

   Owner, on approach to the roadster: "The piracy pop-up has the criteria nicely shown — green when
   distance matches, green when relative speed difference is low enough. … In the boarding dialog just
   seeing green or red told the story much faster." And then: "I guess it could be a general
   meet-the-conditions [display] in the navigation screen."

   So the ROW is the component, not the box. The piracy dossier's autosteal criterion, the conditions strip
   on the Nav screen and anything that grows a two-condition gate later all render THIS, which is what makes
   "all gates speak identically" a fact about the build rather than a promise in a comment.

   IT ADDS NO COLOUR AND NO TYPOGRAPHY. Every class below is one the criterion box already wore before this
   component existed — `text-success` inside the gate, `text-warning` outside it, the ✅/❌ pair — so the
   shipped cascade does not change by a byte and there is no new token for the owner's elegant-minimalism
   ruling to disagree with. That is also why this file has no .razor.css: it has no rules of its own to
   scope, and a rule-less carrier here would only be a file to keep in step.

   THE TREND RIDES ON THE ROW, NOT BESIDE IT (owner: "Are we closing or distancing" · "▲▼ per #210's
   range-rate"). The arrow inherits the row's own colour, so a red chip that is closing reads as one thing
   at a glance and a red chip drifting away reads as another — without a second colour to learn. A criterion
   whose rate nothing in the sim measures draws NO arrow at all rather than a flat one, because a symbol
   that means "we did not work it out" and a symbol that means "it is not moving" must not be the same mark.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *@

<div class="@(Criterion.Inside ? "text-success" : "text-warning")" title="@Hint">
    @(Criterion.Inside ? "✅" : "❌") @Criterion.Label — @Criterion.Reading @Criterion.Gate@Arrow
</div>
